WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.890
Welcome to PyMOL 101 with Molecular Memory!

00:00:02.890 --> 00:00:09.010
I’m KP and in this lesson, we’ll learn
about selection using the protein carbonic

00:00:09.010 --> 00:00:10.730
anhydrase.

00:00:10.730 --> 00:00:14.490
Carbon dioxide is a waste product produced
by our cells.

00:00:14.490 --> 00:00:20.050
Only a small percentage of this gas dissolves
directly in blood plasma, some is carried

00:00:20.050 --> 00:00:28.269
by hemoglobin, but about 70% of it is reacted
by carbonic anhydrase and carried to the lungs

00:00:28.269 --> 00:00:32.189
as water-soluble bicarbonate ions.

00:00:32.189 --> 00:00:38.680
In the lungs, carbonic anhydrase reverses
the reaction, remaking carbon dioxide, which

00:00:38.680 --> 00:00:40.960
we breathe out.

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:45.510
In lesson 1, we learned to load the biological
unit of macromolecules.

00:00:45.510 --> 00:00:55.239
File, get PDB, type 3UYN, set the assembly
to 1, and click download.

00:00:55.239 --> 00:00:58.190
Here’s carbonic anhydrase!

00:00:58.190 --> 00:01:01.390
Display the sequence and we’re ready to
model.

00:01:01.390 --> 00:01:08.229
From the RCSB page, we can access the manuscript
describing this structure.

00:01:08.229 --> 00:01:13.420
Some manuscripts are behind a paywall, but
this is where I found one that gave numbers

00:01:13.420 --> 00:01:18.870
for the important amino acids in this lesson’s
structure.

00:01:18.870 --> 00:01:21.119
Real quick, some terminology.

00:01:21.119 --> 00:01:28.770
An enzyme is a protein that performs a chemical
reaction, making or breaking covalent bonds.

00:01:28.770 --> 00:01:34.210
The active site is the part of an enzyme where
a chemical reaction takes place, and it is

00:01:34.210 --> 00:01:36.630
made up of amino acids.

00:01:36.630 --> 00:01:41.030
When we refer to amino acids in a protein,
we often call them residues.

00:01:41.030 --> 00:01:43.200
Back to business!

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:48.770
We’re going to create and save separate
selections for zinc, the active site residues,

00:01:48.770 --> 00:01:53.390
and the water molecules that interact with
the active site.

00:01:53.390 --> 00:01:59.659
Scrolling through our sequence, we have the
amino acid one-letter codes, a zinc ion, and

00:01:59.659 --> 00:02:01.780
waters.

00:02:01.780 --> 00:02:07.619
Click on zinc, either in the sequence or by
clicking on the gray sphere on the structure

00:02:07.619 --> 00:02:11.400
in the structure viewer window.

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:16.000
The letters sele come up in the names panel.

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:20.840
Click A next to them and choose “rename
selection.”

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:26.510
In the upper left-hand side of the structure
viewer window, a prompt comes up.

00:02:26.510 --> 00:02:30.209
Delete the letters SELE and type a new name.

00:02:30.209 --> 00:02:32.430
I’ll call this “zinc”.

00:02:32.430 --> 00:02:39.000
Now, very important, click somewhere in the
blank space in structure viewer window to

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:45.360
clear the selection, otherwise the next object
you click on will be added to the zinc selection.

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:51.440
Now, let’s select the amino acid residues
that make up carbonic anhydrase’s active

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:53.030
site.

00:02:53.030 --> 00:02:57.130
Using the sequence display, scroll back to
the amino acids.

00:02:57.130 --> 00:03:00.790
I’ll put the list of amino acids we need
on screen.

00:03:00.790 --> 00:03:05.349
First, we’re looking for residue 5.

00:03:05.349 --> 00:03:12.180
Notice that residue 6 is labeled, so residue
5 is this tryptophan just to the left of it.

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:14.909
Click on it to select.

00:03:14.909 --> 00:03:18.519
Next we need residue 7.

00:03:18.519 --> 00:03:26.519
Then N62—be careful, there’s another N,
asparagine, right next to it, so be sure you

00:03:26.519 --> 00:03:28.769
select the correct one.

00:03:28.769 --> 00:03:34.190
K64 next, then H67.

00:03:34.190 --> 00:03:41.660
Now, H94: 91-2-3-4.

00:03:41.660 --> 00:03:44.349
Next up is H96.

00:03:44.349 --> 00:03:49.970
And let’s stop here at histidine 119.

00:03:49.970 --> 00:03:56.099
If it makes it easier for you, you can use
the display dropdown menu to change the sequence

00:03:56.099 --> 00:03:59.310
mode to show three-letter codes.

00:03:59.310 --> 00:04:04.470
I don’t, because I like the way I can see
breaks in the sequence with the one-letter

00:04:04.470 --> 00:04:11.329
codes to easily identify different protein
chains and ligands.

00:04:11.329 --> 00:04:16.389
Notice in the object panel, the letters sele
have popped up again.

00:04:16.389 --> 00:04:18.239
Rename this to “active”.

00:04:18.239 --> 00:04:20.640
A button, rename.

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:25.370
Delete sele and type “active”.

00:04:25.370 --> 00:04:29.479
Click in the blank space of the structure
viewer window to clear the selection.

00:04:29.479 --> 00:04:34.110
But, oh no, we forgot two residues!

00:04:34.110 --> 00:04:39.180
No problem, just click on active again in
the names panel to select it.

00:04:39.180 --> 00:04:42.410
With this selected, we can add the missing
residues.

00:04:42.410 --> 00:04:49.160
Find threonine 199 and 200 in the sequence
and click on them.

00:04:49.160 --> 00:04:50.560
Added!

00:04:50.560 --> 00:04:52.729
Click in the blank space.

00:04:52.729 --> 00:04:56.150
Active site selection complete.

00:04:56.150 --> 00:05:00.180
Selecting active site residues with the command
line is way faster.

00:05:00.180 --> 00:05:04.949
Here’s the command on screen—you can pause
if you’re interested.

00:05:04.949 --> 00:05:09.720
The different ways we represent structures
are called renderings.

00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:15.560
In PyMOL, the protein is shown as a cartoon
rendering by default.

00:05:15.560 --> 00:05:21.560
It makes sense to show our active site residues
in a way that lets us see the atoms, though,

00:05:21.560 --> 00:05:27.220
since they’ll interact with the reactants
as the chemical reaction takes place.

00:05:27.220 --> 00:05:31.020
Add stick rendering to show the active site
residues.

00:05:31.020 --> 00:05:36.710
Next to active in the names panel, click S
for show, and select “sticks”.

00:05:36.710 --> 00:05:43.240
What PyMOL is doing is layering representations
on top of each other.

00:05:43.240 --> 00:05:47.020
Now let’s see what “show as” does.

00:05:47.020 --> 00:05:51.220
Active, S, show as, sticks.

00:05:51.220 --> 00:05:55.130
Woah, parts of the protein cartoon get chopped
out!

00:05:55.130 --> 00:05:57.680
Let’s not do that.

00:05:57.680 --> 00:06:01.530
Click S next to active and add the cartoon
back in.

00:06:01.530 --> 00:06:05.380
Let’s zoom in on the active site.

00:06:05.380 --> 00:06:09.580
Names panel, active, A, zoom.

00:06:09.580 --> 00:06:15.330
Notice how parts of the structure fade—some
clipping has been applied.

00:06:15.330 --> 00:06:19.580
Check out video 1 for a description of clipping
and how to adjust it.

00:06:19.580 --> 00:06:25.240
PyMOL can automatically find the interactions
that polar atoms make.

00:06:25.240 --> 00:06:27.620
Find active in the names panel.

00:06:27.620 --> 00:06:36.150
Click A, find, polar contacts, to any atoms.

00:06:36.150 --> 00:06:39.560
Notice yellow dotted lines show the interactions.

00:06:39.560 --> 00:06:42.050
Let’s do the same for zinc.

00:06:42.050 --> 00:06:48.060
A, find, polar contacts, to any atoms.

00:06:48.060 --> 00:06:53.980
Now we have two different sets of contacts
in the names panel.

00:06:53.980 --> 00:07:00.220
Notice some water molecules have interactions
with the active site amino acid residues and

00:07:00.220 --> 00:07:01.220
zinc.

00:07:01.220 --> 00:07:05.340
Let’s select them by clicking on them in
the structure.

00:07:05.340 --> 00:07:08.360
Rotate and zoom as needed to find them all.

00:07:08.360 --> 00:07:11.360
Now, save this selection.

00:07:11.360 --> 00:07:17.280
A, rename, delete sele, and let’s call this
active water.

00:07:17.280 --> 00:07:22.520
Now, we’ll hide the rest of the water in
the structure.

00:07:22.520 --> 00:07:29.190
Next to 3UYN, click the hide button, H, waters.

00:07:29.190 --> 00:07:33.599
But we do want to show waters in the active
site.

00:07:33.599 --> 00:07:37.680
Next to active water, S, spheres.

00:07:37.680 --> 00:07:40.789
This applies a rendering called spacefill.

00:07:40.789 --> 00:07:45.730
But the waters look huge and might obstruct
parts of the active site.

00:07:45.730 --> 00:07:51.270
Let’s resize them using a preset PyMOL has
built in.

00:07:51.270 --> 00:07:56.879
Next to active_water, A, preset, ball and
stick.

00:07:56.879 --> 00:07:59.560
Ah, that’s much better.

00:07:59.560 --> 00:08:03.800
The interactions look bad because some are
look like they’re going to nowhere.

00:08:03.800 --> 00:08:08.870
They’re actually contacting residues or
waters we haven’t shown.

00:08:08.870 --> 00:08:14.030
I’ll show you how to fix that in an episode
on measurement, but for now let’s just turn

00:08:14.030 --> 00:08:18.150
them off by deselecting them in the names
panel.

00:08:18.150 --> 00:08:24.930
When we display amino acids as sticks, PyMOL
shows the atoms that make up both the backbone

00:08:24.930 --> 00:08:26.370
and side chain.

00:08:26.370 --> 00:08:31.740
The backbone atoms connect the groups that
form new covalent bonds when the protein is

00:08:31.740 --> 00:08:33.270
made.

00:08:33.270 --> 00:08:39.430
The side chain is the variable group—the
R group on that specific amino acid.

00:08:39.430 --> 00:08:42.490
We can show or hide each of these separately.

00:08:42.490 --> 00:08:50.250
So, next to active, select H, main chain.

00:08:50.250 --> 00:08:53.810
To add it back: show, main chain, sticks.

00:08:53.810 --> 00:08:57.100
We’ve done a ton of work!

00:08:57.100 --> 00:09:04.060
Control S to save this PyMOL session to your
computer—we’ll use it in Lesson 3.

00:09:04.060 --> 00:09:05.430
Hope to see you next time!

